<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Lae.Media — everything that talks to the outside world on the media side:
    FFmpeg discovery, capability probing, process launching, pipe reading,
    remote sources (HTTP/SMB), toolchain download and NNEDI3 weights.

    Depends on Lae.Core only. Knows nothing about encoding policy, commands or
    the console.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net11.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Lae.Media</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Lae.Media</AssemblyName>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Lae.Core\Lae.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The translation resources travel inside the assembly, not beside it: v8 ships
    as one self-contained file, and a loose locales/ folder would be the first
    thing missing from a user's download. The logical names are pinned so the
    lookup in I18NextMessages does not depend on MSBuild's folder-to-dots rule.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Localization\locales\en\translation.json" LogicalName="lae.locales.en.translation.json" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Localization\locales\zh\translation.json" LogicalName="lae.locales.zh.translation.json" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Pinned on purpose, and the pin is the whole reason these two lines are not
    one:

    * Microsoft.Data.Sqlite asks for a SQLitePCLRaw version whose bundled native
      library decides the glibc floor. Measured with `objdump -T | grep GLIBC_`:

        2.1.10 -> 2.28   SQLite 3.46.1
        2.1.11 -> 2.28   SQLite 3.49.1   (CVE-2025-6965, fixed in 3.50.2)
        2.1.12 -> 2.34   SQLite 3.53.3
        2.1.13 -> 2.34   SQLite 3.53.3
        2.1.14 -> 2.28   SQLite 3.54.0   <- both constraints at once

      That floor is not academic: 2.34 means the history database fails to load
      on AlmaLinux, Rocky and RHEL 8 (one of this project's two test machines,
      and a normal home-server distribution). 2.1.14 is the only release that
      keeps 2.28 *and* carries a SQLite newer than 3.50.2, which is what
      GHSA-2m69-gcr7-jv3q / CVE-2025-6965 asks for. The advisory still says
      "no patched version" because it tracks the package, not the bundled
      engine.

    * The bundle reference is here so the version that decides that is visible in
      this file. Without it, bumping Microsoft.Data.Sqlite would silently raise
      the glibc floor again, and the failure would show up as a missing .so in a
      user's log rather than as a diff.

    Verified on glibc 2.28 (AlmaLinux 8.10, host) and 2.34 (AlmaLinux 9.8,
    container) before bumping either number.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" Version="10.0.14" />
    <PackageReference Include="SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3" Version="2.1.14" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="I18Next.Net" Version="2.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Tomlyn" Version="2.10.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
